(1 row)


##############################################################################
# 2. Build the incident
##############################################################################
 A nightly batch holds a row lock, then sits idle in its transaction.
 An ALTER TABLE queues behind it, then ordinary reads queue behind the ALTER.

+------+-----+------------------+---------------------+
| sess | pid | application_name |        state        |
+------+-----+------------------+---------------------+
| a    | <PID> | nightly_batch    | idle in transaction |
| b    | <PID> | schema_change    | active              |
| c    | <PID> | report-c         | active              |
| d    | <PID> | report-d         | active              |
| e    | <PID> | report-e         | active              |
+------+-----+------------------+---------------------+
(5 rows)


##############################################################################
# 3. lock_tree_text(): the queue, top to bottom
##############################################################################
 Note reads (report-c/d/e) sit under the ALTER TABLE, not under the batch:
 they queue behind whoever is ahead of them in the lock queue.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                                      lock_tree_text                                                       |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| <PID>  idle in transaction  <AGE>  nightly_batch  UPDATE demo_ledger SET amount = amount WHERE id = 1                          |
| └─<PID>  <AGE>  for AccessExclusiveLock on demo_ledger  relation  BEGIN; SET application_name='schema_change'; ALTER TABLE dem |
|   └─<PID>  <AGE>  for AccessShareLock on demo_ledger  relation  SET application_name='report-c'; SELECT sum(amount) FROM dem   |
|   └─<PID>  <AGE>  for AccessShareLock on demo_ledger  relation  SET application_name='report-d'; SELECT * FROM demo_ledger O   |
|   └─<PID>  <AGE>  for AccessShareLock on demo_ledger  relation  SET application_name='report-e'; SELECT count(*) FROM demo_l   |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
(5 rows)


##############################################################################
# 4. lock_tree: who blocks whom, and for how long
##############################################################################
+------+-------+---------------------+------------------------------------+----------+------------+
| sess | depth |        state        |            waiting_for             |  waited  | blocked_by |
+------+-------+---------------------+------------------------------------+----------+------------+
| a    |     0 | idle in transaction | -                                  | -        | {}         |
| b    |     1 | active              | AccessExclusiveLock on demo_ledger | <AGE> | {<PIDS>}      |
| c    |     2 | active              | AccessShareLock on demo_ledger     | <AGE> | {<PIDS>}      |
| d    |     2 | active              | AccessShareLock on demo_ledger     | <AGE> | {<PIDS>}      |
| e    |     2 | active              | AccessShareLock on demo_ledger     | <AGE> | {<PIDS>}      |
+------+-------+---------------------+------------------------------------+----------+------------+
(5 rows)


##############################################################################
# 5. lock_tree_roots(): the one session to act on
##############################################################################
 One root here: the idle batch, blocking 4 sessions. Everything else is a symptom.

+----------+---------------+---------------------+------------------+-----------+--------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+
| root_pid |      app      |        state        | blocked_sessions | max_depth | longest_wait |                        suggested_action                        |
+----------+---------------+---------------------+------------------+-----------+--------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|      <PID> | nightly_batch | idle in transaction |                4 |         2 | <AGE>     | Idle in an open transaction. SELECT pg_terminate_backend(<PID>); |
+----------+---------------+---------------------+------------------+-----------+--------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+
(1 row)


##############################################################################
# 6. What the operator reads, and what they run
##############################################################################
 Two functions, because they answer different questions. Read the first: it is prose.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                                         lock_tree_actions                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| -- 1 root blocker(s). Most sessions unblocked first.                                                                              |
| -- 1. nightly_batch: idle in transaction, blocks 4 session(s), chain depth 2, longest wait <AGE>                                     |
| -- Descriptions, not commands. Every line here is a comment. For statements you can run: SELECT command FROM lock_tree_command(). |
| -- Terminating a session rolls back its transaction. Re-run the tree afterwards.                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
(4 rows)


 Then run the second. It is statements and nothing else, so it pastes cleanly:

+-----------------------------------+
|              command              |
+-----------------------------------+
| SELECT pg_terminate_backend(<PID>); |
+-----------------------------------+
(1 row)


 Or hand the whole set to psql in one keystroke. It executes every row, so only do this
 once you have read the list above:

     SELECT command FROM lock_tree_command() \gexec

 (not run here, for the obvious reason)

+-----------------------+
| sessions_left_in_tree |
+-----------------------+
|                     0 |
+-----------------------+
(1 row)


##############################################################################
# 7. Bonus: a deadlock has no head of the queue
##############################################################################
 Two sessions each grab one table, then each asks for the other's.
 Nobody is "the blocker", so every session in the cycle is listed as a root.

 Both are hard-blocked on each other. blocked_by shows the cycle:
+-----+----------+-------+--------+------------+-------------------------------+
| pid | root_pid | depth | state  | blocked_by |          waiting_for          |
+-----+----------+-------+--------+------------+-------------------------------+
| <PID> |      <PID> |     0 | active | {<PIDS>}      | AccessExclusiveLock on demo_y |
| <PID> |      <PID> |     0 | active | {<PIDS>}      | AccessExclusiveLock on demo_x |
+-----+----------+-------+--------+------------+-------------------------------+
(2 rows)

 And lock_tree_roots() lists both, with a cancel suggestion for each:
+----------+--------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| root_pid | state  |                                    suggested_action                                     |
+----------+--------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|      <PID> | active | Try SELECT pg_cancel_backend(<PID>); then pg_terminate_backend(<PID>) if it does not clear. |
|      <PID> | active | Try SELECT pg_cancel_backend(<PID>); then pg_terminate_backend(<PID>) if it does not clear. |
+----------+--------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
(2 rows)


##############################################################################
# 8. Bonus: what it looked like while you were not looking
##############################################################################
 Everything above describes the present. History records the rest: what you read
 when an incident is noticed AFTER it ended and pg_stat_activity has moved on.

 In production you schedule the sample (pg_cron, or any scheduler) and it runs on
 its own. Here, three samples taken by hand and backdated, so they read as three
 separate incidents:

+----------+---------+---------------+------------+--------------+-------+-----------------+
| incident | samples | peak_sessions | peak_depth | longest_wait | roots |  applications   |
+----------+---------+---------------+------------+--------------+-------+-----------------+
|        3 |       1 |             3 |          2 | <AGE>     | {<PIDS>} | {nightly_batch} |
|        2 |       1 |             2 |          1 | <AGE>     | {<PIDS>} | {nightly_batch} |
|        1 |       1 |             3 |          2 | <AGE>     | {<PIDS>} | {nightly_batch} |
+----------+---------+---------------+------------+--------------+-------+-----------------+
(3 rows)


 Three incidents from samples an hour apart. That is the answer to "it was hanging
 earlier": when, how bad, and which application was holding the lock.

 Per-query blocking, the two ways of asking. Both read the same three samples and
 they report different units, which is the reason both exist:

+---------------------+-----------------+------------------+------+---------------+-----------------+
|      query_id       | session_samples | blocking_seconds | gaps | gaps_excluded |  applications   |
+---------------------+-----------------+------------------+------+---------------+-----------------+
| <PID> |               3 |         9600.000 |    2 |             0 | {nightly_batch} |
+---------------------+-----------------+------------------+------+---------------+-----------------+
(1 row)


 The same time, per application. Not a re-grouping of the row above: where an
 application hands the queue from one statement to another, the per-query figure
 throws that gap away and this one counts it, so summing the per-query seconds
 UNDERSTATES a service whose statements take turns blocking.

+------------------+------------------+-----------------------+----------------+--------------+-----------------------+
| application_name | blocking_seconds | blocked_sessions_peak | sessions_total | longest_wait |       query_ids       |
+------------------+------------------+-----------------------+----------------+--------------+-----------------------+
| nightly_batch    |         9600.000 |                     2 |              5 | <AGE>     | {<PIDS>} |
+------------------+------------------+-----------------------+----------------+--------------+-----------------------+
(1 row)


 The samples above are 3 hours, 2 hours and 20 minutes old, so the two gaps between
 them are 1 hour and 40 minutes, and <PID> seconds is exactly those two added up. Two
 things it is not doing, and both are deliberate:

   It is not counting the last incident at all. Nothing was sampled after it, so
     nobody observed whether the query was still holding the lock. A lower bound.
   It is not apportioning the gap between the second and third incidents. Both
     ends have the same root here, so the whole gap counts; had the root changed
     mid-gap, the gap would be discarded instead, because nobody observed when.

 The history can also be LOOKED AT, not only queried. The tree these samples
 recorded, replayed in the same layout lock_tree_text() uses:

+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                         lock_tree_replay_text                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| <PID>  idle in transaction  nightly_batch  UPDATE ledger SET amount = amount WHERE id = 1               |
| └─<PID>  <AGE>  for AccessExclusiveLock on demo_ledger  UPDATE ledger SET amount = amount WHERE id = 1  |
|   └─<PID>  <AGE>  for AccessExclusiveLock on demo_ledger  UPDATE ledger SET amount = amount WHERE id = 1 |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
(3 rows)


 Nine columns of lock_tree are not in a replay, and that is deliberate. path and
 blocked_by cannot be reconstructed: the history holds one row per session, not
 the edge between them. The rest were never recorded at all.

 The waiting_for above is unqualified only because this demo INSERTED its history
 by hand rather than sampling it. A real lock_tree_sample() runs with search_path
 set to pg_catalog, so regclass cannot resolve unqualified there and every row it
 writes is schema-qualified. That is the stable rendering, and the live view's is
 the path-dependent one. See test/18_replay.sql.

 And while nothing is blocked, sampling writes nothing at all:

+----------------------------------+
| rows_written_on_a_quiet_database |
+----------------------------------+
|                                0 |
+----------------------------------+
(1 row)


